# Article: Project

This project is **swarm-forge-herdr itself** — the swarm runtime you are
running on. You are building your own next phase. Precision matters
doubly: broken runtime code breaks future swarms, including this one.

## Language and layout

- Language: Babashka (Clojure) scripts + bash wrappers
- Runtime scripts: `swarmforge/scripts/` (the handoff protocol, router)
- Launcher: `bin/swarm`
- Stock prompts/contracts: `prompts/` (installed copies live under
  `swarmforge/roles`, `swarmforge/contracts` — edit `prompts/` as source
  of truth and sync the installed copy when a task touches both)
- Tests: `test/smoke.sh` (end-to-end protocol suite)
- Design authority for v2 work: `docs/squad-v2.md` — follow it exactly;
  deviations are findings to report, not decisions to make.

## Commands

- Test suite: `./test/smoke.sh` (must end `SMOKE PASSED (N checks)`)
- Syntax check a script: `bb <file>` with args, or load it —
  every `.bb` you touch must at least parse
- No coverage/mutation tooling for bb; the smoke suite and adversarial
  review are the quality gates

## Quality bars

- Smoke suite green before any handoff; new behavior needs new smoke
  checks in the same commit.
- Follow existing code style: one shared lib, thin entries, agent-legible
  error tokens, docstrings on non-obvious fns.
- The running daemon/scripts must stay protocol-compatible: never change
  existing output tokens, exit codes, or file formats without the task
  explicitly saying so.

## Notes

- Commit attribution: none. This project wants no `Co-Authored-By:` and
  no other trailer or footer on commits — there is no attribution hook
  installed, and the swarm's own audit trail already records authorship.
- Do not edit `.swarmforge/` runtime state or restart daemons; the human
  operator owns the running swarm.
